--- body ---
## Context

Author directive, 2026-10-27: management by intention, under KISS, less is
more, and Trust, Verify, Monitor. The orchestrator says what to verify and
why; gaze owns the how. This replaces the brief grammar of the 2026-10-26
version of this ticket (PR 1686, closed unmerged: too much ceremony, a
170-line resolver and a seat-manifest check policing the caller's wording).

Why the earlier attempts did not take. Attempt 1 (tracker [[1100]]): the
caller wrote the review plan, under a risk floor. Attempt 2 (ticket 1176):
the orchestrator was to choose the seats, but nothing sized them, so the full
panel ran by default. Attempt 3 (PR
1686): the caller wrote seats and scope in a grammar. Here the caller writes
only intent; the what bounds the scope, the why sets the risk, gaze plans.

Evidence (2026-10-26 night): PR 1677, about 200 lines, took four full panels;
PR 1676 took thirteen gaze rounds, most re-reading 1,200 lines to check a fix
of a few lines. Ticket 1197's measurement (docs/2026-10-27-gaze-wall-time.md):
setup and closing turns are 42 percent of gaze wall time; a narrower panel
saves at most 32 s per run, so the gain sought here is fewer and smaller
rounds, not a faster round.

## Design

1. **Intent in.** Free text after the merge request number, in the shape
   `/gaze <pr> verify <what> because <why>`; the parser stays lenient. No
   intent: the what is the whole merge request, the why its ticket's exit
   criteria. Nothing is refused for its wording.
2. **Plan.** Gaze writes a short plan before launching anything: method,
   workers, budget. It derives them from the change's nature (prose or code),
   size, location (the risk rows of skills/route/references/decorrelation.md)
   and history (what earlier results on this merge request covered). The
   cheapest method that settles the what wins: running a test against old and
   new code, one seat, a panel, an external or other-family model, a weaker
   or stronger model, chosen through the route skill. For a high-risk what
   (the decorrelation.md rows: rules, gates, credentials, deletion), a look
   from another model family is worth its cost; gaze weighs that by judgement.
   Mechanical lints (adherence tests, AI-tells for prose) are checks the plan
   runs as scripts, never review seats.
3. **Execute.** Exactly the plan, nothing added.
4. **Verify.** Plan against what was done: each planned item ran and
   reported; each worker is the family planned (its launch line, and the
   worker's own first line, where it names its model and family); the run covered the
   stated what; the budget held. A deviation is named with its reason. A
   planned perspective missing without a substitute that still settles the
   what means the verdict cannot be REVIEWED. There is no retry: a worker
   that timed out or could not be reached is a deviation, reported so the
   orchestrator can decide.
5. **Record.** One result comment: intent, plan, what ran (worker, family,
   model when known, time), plan check, findings, verdict (REVIEWED, FINDINGS
   or ESCALATE; gaze never approves), duration. The interface stays: the
   fields `/verify-gate`, `/merge` and `/raid` read today (`reviewed_head_sha`,
   `adherence`, the result line) keep their names; `plan check:` replaces
   `panel_integrity:`, and its readers are repointed in the same change. The
   reviewer `attribution` block in the merge request body stays, as the
   register of who reviewed (read by `/merge` and the journal); the result
   comment is the register of what was found.

Properties of every run:

- **One pass, no recursion.** The pass may be multi-headed: several workers
  in parallel, as the plan says. It does not recurse: no worker launches
  another review, gaze does not review its own findings again, and there is
  no internal round or fix loop. Findings go back to the caller, who fixes
  and, if it wants, sends a new narrower intent.
- **Read-only on the code.** Gaze never edits, commits or pushes on the
  merge request's branch, nor writes in the caller's worktree.
- **Checks allowed, scratch clean.** Gaze may run tests or scripts to settle
  the what, in a throwaway worktree of its own; it removes that worktree and
  every scratch file before returning. The existing cleanup step
  (`skills/gaze/references/cleanup.md`, with its exit preflight that refuses
  to remove a tree holding real work) already enforces this; keep it.
- **One delivery place.** The report is the merge request's gaze result
  comment, the same text returned to the caller, plus the reviewer lines in
  the body's attribution block.

Trust: the orchestrator trusts gaze's plan; gaze trusts the coder's tests and
the gate's checks. Verify: step 4, and the comment shows intent beside plan.
Monitor: trace-doctor and the 1197 telemetry judge plan sizing across runs;
guidance changes on that evidence, never through a per-run floor.

Deleted as cruft: `--force-approve` (nine uses, each to escape review
rounds or to say a docs-only change needed little review; one pass and a
narrow intent say that without a special path); the one-retry rule; the
`battery:`, `review_groups:` and `multi_ticket:` result lines, which nothing
outside gaze acts on except tests that pin them (`battery: NOT-RUN` for
detached substitution becomes a plan-check deviation).

A later round is a new, narrower intent ("verify the fixes for findings X
and Y"), not a re-run of the whole review. Memory files every session reads
(ticket 1223, folded in earlier) are covered the same way: an orchestrator
that wants them reviewed says so in the intent.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `/gaze` takes optional free text after the merge request number as its intent (what, why); with none, the default intent is the whole merge request against its ticket's exit criteria.
- [ ] Gaze writes its plan (method, workers, budget) before launching anything, from nature, size, location and history; the guidance for it lives in one place and states the "cheapest method that settles the what" rule with a few worked cases.
- [ ] After executing, gaze compares plan with what was done and records the outcome as a `plan check:` line; a planned perspective missing without a valid substitute blocks a REVIEWED verdict.
- [ ] The result comment carries intent, plan, run, plan check, findings, verdict and duration; `reviewed_head_sha`, `adherence` and the result line keep their names; `plan check:` replaces `panel_integrity:` with every reader (verify-gate, merge, raid, tests) repointed; the body's attribution block is still written.
- [ ] One pass, no recursion: the plan may run several workers in parallel, but no worker launches a further review and gaze has no internal round or fix loop; it returns after the single pass.
- [ ] Read-only: a test shows a gaze run leaves the merge request's branch tip and the caller's worktree unchanged.
- [ ] Scratch clean: checks run in gaze's own throwaway worktree; the existing cleanup step and exit preflight stay and are the check.
- [ ] `/hunt`, `/raid` and `/merge` pass an intent and drop their fixed gaze round loops: a later round is a new, narrower intent. The gate's two-round cap is unchanged.
- [ ] Codex and Pi: the intent is passed verbatim to the detached gaze session (`skills/raid/references/detached-gaze.md`).
- [ ] Deleted: the fixed battery as a default (`references/battery.md`), `--force-approve`, the one-retry rule, the `battery:`, `review_groups:` and `multi_ticket:` lines, and the per-phase integrity rules the plan check replaces. `skills/gaze/SKILL.md` plus its references lose lines net, the count stated in the merge request; the plan guidance is one file, and a search finds its "cheapest method" rule nowhere else.
- [ ] Regression tests: a small intent yields a one-worker plan; a plan item that did not run makes the plan check fail and blocks REVIEWED.
- [ ] The harness gate (`.merge-gate.json` checks) passes.
